  "query_preview": "..Much quieter tonight than 24 hours ago with just\nsome mid to high level clouds over southeast Arizona. High \npressure is still centered over northern New Mexico but will \nmove further west and strengthen Thursday into Friday. PWAT \nvalues are right around normal for this time of year and will \nremain that way through the weekend. \n\nToday looks to be a bit more active than Tuesday with hi-res\nguidance showing scattered convection south and east of Tucson\nthis afternoon and evening. A couple HREF members show isolated\nactivity over Tucson, but the metro and central Pima County will\nlikely be on the western periphery of storm chances. CAPE values\nwill be around 1000 J/kg with modest 0-6 km shear of 15-25 kts\nfrom Tucson eastward. Expect normal monsoon hazards with some\nstorms capable of severe wind gusts thanks to the wind shear. \nLocally heavy rainfall will also be a threat with HREF and REFS \nshowing the southern half of Cochise County seeing the best \nchances for heavy rain. \n\nThursday into Friday the ridge begins to move westward into\nArizona and may suppress storm activity. Surface moisture also \ndecreases slightly both days with dewpoints dropping into the \nlow 50s. Expect isolated activity both days mainly along the \nAZ/NM border and the White Mountains. The main story will be the\nvery hot temperatures. Highs will run 7-12 degrees above normal\nThursday through Sunday with Tucson seeing a good chance to hit\n110 degrees for the first time this year. NBM probabilities for\n110 degrees or higher at Tucson international are 15 percent \nThursday, 55 percent Friday, 70 percent Saturday, and 40 percent\nSunday. These temperatures will lead to Major and Extreme \nHeatRisk and an Extreme Heat Warning is in effect Thursday \nthrough Sunday. \n\nThis weekend storm chances slowly increase in coverage, but the\nbest chances will still be to the south and east of Tucson. This\ntrend continues into early next week with the potential for an \nuptick in thunderstorm activity as the ridge decreases sightly \nin strength and becomes centered closer to the Four Corners.",
